-I have spent years watching the same crystalline structures form in different laboratories, on different continents, under different hands. The first synthesis of a novel compound is always the hardest. The second is easier. The tenth is routine. By the hundredth, graduate students perform the procedure half-asleep, and the crystals assemble themselves with an almost irritable efficiency. This is not a metaphor. It is a measurement. Rupert Sheldrake called it morphic resonance — the idea that nature remembers, and that memory is not stored in genes or brains but in fields that behave like standing waves across time.
